127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (This paper examines how the meaning and function of the archive have shifted in the context of the digital age. Once understood as a stable and institutionally controlled physical space containing reliable records, archives have transformed into accessible systems that exist through digital technologies and online platforms. By examining archival theory and contemporary artists that engage with archival material, this paper argues that traditional archives cannot be understood as a neutral or objective source of historical truth. Instead, they operate as systems of power that shape historical narratives and determine which voices are preserved.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (While the internet has enabled broader access and participation in archival creation, it produced instability, algorithmic control and the loss of material aura. These conditionals challenge the foundational concepts of archives such as permanence, authority and objectivity. Through the analysis of case studies, this research shows how artists respond to these limitations by developing unconventional archives. Instead of replicating traditional archival models, artists introduce additional narratives, experiment with viewer experience and technologies, redistribute authorship and treat the archives as a separate medium. Unconventional archives thus offer a more accurate reflection of informational society that relies on constant data production and its circulation. These archives have adapted to the modern needs, and created space for artistic and cultural expression. )
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 104 0 R
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E (When thinking of an archive, one can imagine an old room, shelves filled with books and files, securely hidden from the outside world, where the content has been selected and placed there by a person or institution for a particular purpose. Access to the archive is limited to specific researchers or scientists. These places are considered to be a reliable source of information, records and materials. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E (With the creation of the internet, the idea of an archive has shifted from its original meaning. Websites such as The Internet Archive make material accessible for any user to view and download. Wikipedia allows users not only to view the information but also to modify and add their own text or images. The archive is no longer created by a single authority but becomes a collective process. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E (This paper focuses on what could be understood as an archive today. Arguing that, in the age of the internet, the archive has shifted from being an institutionally controlled source of information to a public structure where anyone can produce, alter and publish records. While this democratization of information allows visibility and open access, it also weakens the reliability of the archive as a source of truth. Contemporary artists engage with archives, where the intention is not to bring objectivity and truth back but to show the inherent nature, biases and power, and the archive becomes an artistic medium itself. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 105 0 R
/T (Defining an Archive)
/E (Defining an Archive)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (Before exploring the idea of unconventional archives it is first important to understand what a traditional archive is and what role it is plays in the society. Archives consist of a set of records and documents that are stored in a physical space or are available on the internet for open or restricted access \(Cambridge Dictionary, 2025\). Unlike libraries that consist of multiple copies of the material, archival material is normally unique and stored as originals. The records come in different formats such as text, photographs, video, sound, analogue and digital. They are held by individuals and institutions, both public and private, such as individuals, families, museums, universities, states, corporations \(International Council on Archives 2025\). )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 105 0 R
/T (Historical use of Archives)
/E (Historical use of Archives)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (Historically, archives fulfil the need to collect, preserve and pass information across generations. In order to be transmitted, the culture needs to be recorded and stored somewhere. Otherwise, the stories that are just told orally will not survive\(Carter, R.G.S., 2006\). Archives function as a form of collective and personal memory, where communities and individuals are able to remember their past, maintain and form a shared identity and build a strong community.  )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (However, this notion of objectivity also brings power. Before the modern era, to be a subject of archival documentation was a privilege for those in the position of power. Their life and actions were preserved in relic-like fashion, granting authority, visibility and the right to shape historical narratives \(Laermans, Gielen, 2007\). Instead of pure documentations, this determined whose voices were heard, and whose claims could be supported with evidence. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
140 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (In contrast, in the contemporary informational society, the relationship between archives and power has transformed. The production of information no longer depends on institutions and elites. Individuals continuously create and circulate content through cameras, digital platforms and social media, while simultaneously producing data through participating in this digital system. Information functions as a tool of expression and mechanism through which society can be monitored and controlled \(Laermans, Gielen, 2007\). )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0 1]
>>
endobj
141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (For digital archives, the power is redistributed instead of fully disappearing. While the material is produced and circulated by users, instead of just institutional authorities, the visibility of archival content is controlled by the digital system. Accessibility and long-term preservation largely depend on digital platforms, search engines and algorithms. Even when users create the material, they do not have full control how it is distributed, sustained and made visible. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (It is impossible for the archives to stay entirely truthful and objective, the process of selection inevitably produces gaps within the archival material. The gaps occur when some perspectives, individuals or groups are being ignored, silenced or neglected. These gaps may cause a negative impact. For example, as Amcher states in Archival Silences, marginalised groups who do not have their past documented in the archives properly, may end up unable to argue their case, to seek accountability, claim their rights and thus may be further victimized. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3 4 5]
>>
endobj
143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Before the material gets into the archives, archivists have the power to manage the records, their selection, descriptions, preservation, use and presentation within the archive. These decisions determine how the past is being remembered and how it will be reinterpreted in the future. As the International Council on Archives states, archival records should not be viewed as an objective truth, they represent specific perspectives shaped by the worldview and experiences of entities producing them, while viewers' context and experience are equally as important for the interpretation of archival resources \(International Council on Archives, 2025\). Archivisation does not simply document the past but actively produces it, includes )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
144 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (specific perspectives, while excluding others. This results in the way society understands their past, and which stories are privileged and which are silenced\(Carter, R.G.S., 2006\).)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 107 0 R
/T (Digital Archives)
/E (Digital Archives)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (With technological advances, contemporary society has easy access to tools of documentation such as smartphones, digital cameras, 3D scanning applications and internet-based platforms. The internet allows a vast variety of media to be constantly produced, shared and stored, resulting in distribution of large amounts of documentation that lives outside of traditional archival institutions. This creates an opportunity for any individual or group to create their own archives without relying on institutional control.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (While the possibility for anyone to create an archive introduces new challenges, such as misinformation and instability, it reflects contemporary society more accurately than selective institutional models from the past. These archives emerge from everyday contributions of users with different viewpoints, instead of a few numbers of authoritative figures. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
149 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (In the digital age, often described as the breakthrough of information society, the media and content is being constantly shared, added, changed and transformed. The same conditions apply to digital archives. Laermans and Gielen in The archive of the digital an-archive, )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
150 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0 1]
>>
endobj
151 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
152 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
