/P 108 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5 6 7]
>>
endobj
154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (This transformation is further continued with the digital archives. As Laermans and Gielen argue, digital archival material exists fundamentally as data, as ones and zeros, which can be accessed only through specific programs, interfaces and devices. The way the material appears and interpreted depends fully on the technological system through which it is being accessed. The archive does not exist outside of screens, software and interfaces. In the future, as technologies continue to evolve, devices and software will be updated or become completely different. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 109 0 R
/T (As a result, digital archives will require constant adaptation, or complete transformation in order to be accessible. Fundamentally challenging the idea of static long term preservation. )
/E (As a result, digital archives will require constant adaptation, or complete transformation in order to be accessible. Fundamentally challenging the idea of static long term preservation. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0 1]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 109 0 R
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 109 0 R
/T (While digital archives are not easily suited for long-term preservation, in a similar way as traditional ones, the instability is not necessarily their negative side. With appropriate transformation and updating, digital content can remain accessible even if its form changes. As digital archives have become a new medium, their purpose does not lie only in preservation but in the ability to comment on, reinterpret and recontextualise the material and the past. )
/E (While digital archives are not easily suited for long-term preservation, in a similar way as traditional ones, the instability is not necessarily their negative side. With appropriate transformation and updating, digital content can remain accessible even if its form changes. As digital archives have become a new medium, their purpose does not lie only in preservation but in the ability to comment on, reinterpret and recontextualise the material and the past. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 110 0 R
/T (Redefining an archive )
/E (Redefining an archive )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (The limitations of traditional archives discussed above does not mean a complete failure of traditional archival models. Instead, it indicates a mismatch between the established archival principle and the conditions of the digital age. Traditional archives exist in permanence, stability and institutional authority, while contemporary society is shaped by constant technological change, network and distributed authorship.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (In response to this shift, alternative archives have emerged. They do not attempt to be objective, static or long lasting and replicate traditional models. Instead they are embracing constant change, subjectivity and technological advancement. These archives, referred to as unconventional archives, use digital tools to collect, reorganise and present material in ways that reflect contemporary culture and knowledge production. Unconventional archives are often created by individuals, artists or online collectives, and they recontextualise existing records, add personal or marginalised narratives, and treat the archive itself as a creative medium. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (An unconventional archive includes main functions of a traditional archive, such as collecting, organising and presenting material. Each of these functions are rethought for the purpose of the archive. Instead of being created by institutional authority, unconventional archives are created by individuals, groups or internet collectives. They are presented and published through digital platforms, interactive environments, VR, websites, code based websites, and can take the form of a research project or an artwork. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (Unconventional archives differ from traditional archives in several key ways; most importantly in who collects the material, for what purpose, how the content is structured, and how it is accessed. Instead of trying to achieve objective truth the archive may be grounding in subjectivity, the process of archivisation itself, and the use of materials from multiple sources. In this context, the archive can be understood not only for its content but as a separate creative work. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
