396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 106 0 R
/T (Gaps in Traditional Archives)
/E (Gaps in Traditional Archives)
/Pg 12 0 R
>>
endobj
397 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 12 0 R
>>
endobj
398 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (Even when the archivists and viewers are aware of the material selection and occurring gaps, traditional archives stay limited by institutional control, physical access, and established power structure. Some communities or individuals may not wish to be included in the state or institutional archives, while others may be actively silenced by those in power. This represents an issue of who has the power to document history and whose memories are preserved as part of the official record.)
/Pg 13 0 R
>>
endobj
399 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 108 0 R
/T (What is lost through digitalization)
/E (What is lost through digitalization)
/Pg 14 0 R
>>
endobj
400 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 109 0 R
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
>>
endobj
401 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 109 0 R
/T (The loss of material context of the original objects allows archival material to be rethought, reinterpreted and restructured. When approaching critically, digital archives can function not as weakened copies of the traditional ones, but as a new medium that is capable of creating new narratives, archival experience and artistic expressions. Instead of aiming for objectivity, they work by transformation, participation and technological advancement, offering an archival practice the aligns with the conditions of contemporary technological society.)
/E (The loss of material context of the original objects allows archival material to be rethought, reinterpreted and restructured. When approaching critically, digital archives can function not as weakened copies of the traditional ones, but as a new medium that is capable of creating new narratives, archival experience and artistic expressions. Instead of aiming for objectivity, they work by transformation, participation and technological advancement, offering an archival practice the aligns with the conditions of contemporary technological society.)
/Pg 15 0 R
>>
endobj
402 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (In this paper, unconventional archives are examined through a set of interconnected criteria which determine them. They include the content of the archive and the motivation behind its collection; the structure and recontextualisation of material, where the original records are being transformed, rethought and reorganised to layer additional narratives; authorship, which determines who creates and adds the material to the archive; and viewer experience and presentation, focusing on the platform, interface and technology through which the archive is accessed. Although the criteria is described separately, it often overlaps within each example discussed in the case studies.)
/Pg 16 0 R
>>
endobj
403 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 17 0 R
>>
endobj
404 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
>>
endobj
405 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 113 0 R
/T (At first glance, the Museum of Stolen Art resembles a traditional archive that has simply been digitalised. It stores and presents historical objects, while providing information and aims to preserve cultural memory. However, the digital format allows the archive to function in ways and mediums that are unavailable for physical institutional space. Artists used different techniques of storing and presenting the artifacts, such as 3D scans, flying texts, use of visual effects, and spatial alignment of the museum. The artefacts now are being re-presented through digital location, animations and interactions. The content of the archive is not only focusing on the specific objects themselves, but explores the way a viewer is experiencing them individually and as a collection in the digital space.)
/E (At first glance, the Museum of Stolen Art resembles a traditional archive that has simply been digitalised. It stores and presents historical objects, while providing information and aims to preserve cultural memory. However, the digital format allows the archive to function in ways and mediums that are unavailable for physical institutional space. Artists used different techniques of storing and presenting the artifacts, such as 3D scans, flying texts, use of visual effects, and spatial alignment of the museum. The artefacts now are being re-presented through digital location, animations and interactions. The content of the archive is not only focusing on the specific objects themselves, but explores the way a viewer is experiencing them individually and as a collection in the digital space.)
/Pg 19 0 R
>>
endobj
406 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 116 0 R
/T (The act of 3D scanning takes more time than simply taking a photograph. The artist's choice to 3D scan these spaces and publish them on a code-based website, allowing users to rotate and inspect them closely, suggests that significance is produced through archival framing rather than the initial value of the subject.)
/E (The act of 3D scanning takes more time than simply taking a photograph. The artist's choice to 3D scan these spaces and publish them on a code-based website, allowing users to rotate and inspect them closely, suggests that significance is produced through archival framing rather than the initial value of the subject.)
/Pg 22 0 R
>>
endobj
407 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 117 0 R
/E (The authorship is distributed around thousands of users. The artists were the creators of the original idea and the themes, but did not have as much control over each individual's contribution. The collective authorship represented how multiplicity of voices can coexist within the same archive.)
/Pg 23 0 R
>>
endobj
408 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 118 0 R
/T (Conclusion)
/E (Conclusion)
/Pg 24 0 R
>>
endobj
409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 118 0 R
/E (By examining archival theory and contemporary artistic practices, this paper has argued that traditional archives cannot be understood as neutral tools for storing objective truth. Instead, it functions as a system of power that shapes historical narratives, determines which voices are heard and produces archival gaps. While the internet allows for participation and democratizes access to information, it also brings instability, algorithmic control and loss of material aura. These conditions contradict the traditional archival ideas of permanence and objectivity.)
/Pg 24 0 R
>>
endobj
